          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have osteoarthritis in both knees.",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a long-term joint condition. According to the General clinical reference on osteoarthritis (condition-osteoarthritis), structured exercise is the core treatment for every affected joint. That exercise combines muscle strengthening, range-of-movement work and aerobic activity. Weight management is also part of treatment where relevant, and the document says education and pacing help too.\n\nThe same document says the condition is manageable over long periods and does not inevitably lead to joint replacement. It also notes that pain and reduced activity can affect sleep, mood and fitness, and that inactivity can make these worse.\n\nThe search returned only this one general passage. It does not cover knee-specific treatments, which options suit which people, or when to see a clinician. For questions about your own knees, such as what treatment fits you or how to manage flare-ups, please talk with your doctor or a physiotherapist.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
