          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is walking good or bad for them?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched do not say directly whether walking is good or bad for knee osteoarthritis.\n\nThey do say that structured exercise is the main treatment for knee osteoarthritis, and that aerobic activity is one of its three parts, alongside muscle strengthening and range-of-movement work. This comes from the Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management guidance (guideline-osteoarthritis-management) and the General clinical reference on osteoarthritis exercise note (lifestyle-osteoarthritis-activity). Those documents also say exercise helps regardless of how worn the joint looks or how much pain there is at the start. They say pain during exercise within tolerable limits is not a sign of damage. They also recommend starting with supervision, because the right movements and gradual progression matter.\n\nNeither document says which walking routes, distances or paces suit knees. The search also returned a general note on walking as moderate activity, but it was about blood pressure, not knees, so I have not used it here.\n\nPlease ask your doctor or a physiotherapist what kind and amount of walking suits your knees, and how to tell when activity is helping rather than causing problems.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 5720.612995999545,
          "red_flag_matched": false,
          "red_flag_matched_raw": false,
